  "statement": "You're given an m x n grid of integers, `matrix`. Wherever a cell holds 0, set every cell in its row and in its column to 0.\n\nDo it in place: change `matrix` itself and return nothing. Only the zeros that were in the grid before your function started count; zeros that you write along the way must not cause more rows and columns to be wiped. Remembering the zero rows and columns in extra sets works; the interesting challenge is doing it with constant extra space.\n\nConstraints: 1 <= m, n <= 50; -1000 <= matrix[i][j] <= 1000.",
  "kind": "function",
  "functionName": "set_zeroes",
  "signature": "def set_zeroes(matrix: List[List[int]]) -> None:",
  "starterCode": "def set_zeroes(matrix: List[List[int]]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"If a cell is 0, set its whole row and column to 0, in place.\"\"\"\n    pass\n",
